course: books
lesson: 42
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter V, part 2: Dulce Domum (the Mole's sobs, the Rat turns back, and Mole End)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The Rat chatters away about the fire and the supper waiting at home and doesn't notice how quiet the Mole has gone. At last he suggests a rest on some tree-stumps by a copse (a small wood) — and the sob the Mole has been holding back bursts out, then another and another, until he is crying openly.
explain: Between sobs it all comes out: his shabby little home, how he forgot it, how he smelt it on the road, how the Rat wouldn't turn back. The Rat says nothing for a while, just pats his shoulder. Then he mutters: «What a pig I have been! A pig—that's me!»
explain: When the sobs calm down, the Rat simply gets up and walks back the way they came. «We're going to find that home of yours,» he says. The Mole protests — too late, too dark, and the Rat's supper! «Hang River Bank, and supper too!» says the Rat, and takes his arm.
explain: Near the spot, the Rat lets go and waits. The Mole's nose quivers… a quick run, a stop, a try back, then a sure advance — over a ditch, through a hedge, across a field, and down a tunnel. The Mole strikes a match: there is a little front door with «Mole End» painted over the bell-pull, a tidy forecourt, a goldfish pond with a shining glass ball that reflects everything all wrong.
fact: The Rat chatters «about what they would do when they got back» — «how jolly a fire of logs in the parlour would be» — «never noticing his companion's silence and distressful state of mind».
fact: The Rat: «Look here, Mole old chap, you seem dead tired. No talk left in you, and your feet dragging like lead.» (Dead tired = very tired; feet like lead = feet heavy as metal.)
fact: «The sob he had fought with so long refused to be beaten … poor Mole at last gave up the struggle, and cried freely and helplessly and openly».
fact: The Mole: «I know it's a—shabby, dingy little place … not like—your cosy quarters—or Toad's beautiful hall—or Badger's great house—but it was my own little home—and I was fond of it».
fact: «We might have just gone and had one look at it, Ratty—only one look—it was close by—but you wouldn't turn back, Ratty, you wouldn't turn back!»
fact: The Rat: «I see it all now! What a pig I have been! A pig—that's me! Just a pig—a plain pig!» (Here «pig» means a selfish, thoughtless person.)
fact: He waits till «sniffs were frequent and sobs only intermittent» (intermittent = coming now and then), then says «carelessly» that they'd better be getting on, and sets off the way they came.
fact: «Wherever are you (hic) going to (hic), Ratty?» — the Mole still has the hiccups from crying. The Rat: «We're going to find that home of yours, old fellow … we shall want your nose.»
fact: The Mole: «It's too late, and too dark, and the place is too far off, and the snow's coming!» The Rat: «Hang River Bank, and supper too! … I'm going to find this place now, if I stay out all night.»
fact: Near the spot the Rat feels «a faint sort of electric thrill» through his arm. «The signals were coming through!» Then: «a short, quick run forward—a fault—a check—a try back; and then a slow, steady, confident advance» (words from hunting dogs following a scent).
fact: The Mole moves «with something of the air of a sleep-walker», crosses a dry ditch, scrambles through a hedge, crosses a field, and dives down a tunnel.
fact: The front door has «“Mole End” painted, in Gothic lettering, over the bell-pull» (Gothic lettering = old-fashioned, pointed letters).
fact: The forecourt has a garden-seat, a roller (the Mole hates his ground «kicked up» into little runs), ferns in wire baskets, and plaster statues of «Garibaldi, and the infant Samuel, and Queen Victoria, and other heroes of modern Italy» — a joke, because only Garibaldi was an Italian hero.
fact: There is a skittle-alley (skittles = a game like bowling), and a little pond with gold-fish, a cockle-shell border, and in its middle a little pile of more cockle-shells topped by «a large silvered glass ball that reflected everything all wrong and had a very pleasing effect».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bostin'» (Black Country, England) = great; «tara-a-bit» (Black Country) = see you later; «slay» = do it brilliantly; «salty» = cross, annoyed; «lekker» (South Africa) = nice, great.
exercise: What does the Rat chatter about as they walk on? | The fire, the supper, what they'll do at home | The Badger's old city and the people who built it | Toad's new car | The snow
exercise: What does the Rat NOT notice? | The Mole's silence and sadness | The snow beginning to fall heavily on them both | The way home | The village
exercise: Where do they sit down to rest? | On tree-stumps by a copse | On a bench in the village churchyard | On the bank of the frozen river | On a stile at the edge of a field
exercise: Why does the Rat suggest a rest? | The Mole seems dead tired, his feet dragging | He has a stone in his boot and needs to take it out | It is snowing | He is hungry
exercise: What happens when the Mole sits down? | His held-back sob bursts out, and he cries openly | He falls fast asleep on the stump at once | He laughs | He runs away
exercise: How does the Mole describe his home? | Shabby and dingy — but his own | A fine, big, splendid house, like Toad Hall | Cold and horrible | He doesn't mention it
exercise: Which three homes does the Mole compare his own with? | The Rat's, Toad's and Badger's | The Otter's, the hedgehogs' and the rabbits' | The village cottages | Only the Rat's
exercise: «We might have just gone and had one look at it.» How many looks did the Mole want? | Only one | Two or three, to be sure it was there | A whole night's stay | A whole week
exercise: What does the Rat do while the Mole is sobbing? | Says nothing, just pats his shoulder | Tells him loudly to stop being a baby | Walks on alone | Sings to cheer him up
exercise: What does the Rat call himself? | A pig | A fool and an ass, like the Mole in the boat | A silly old rat | A wicked beast
exercise: When does the Rat get up to go? | When the sobs are only now and then | At once, before the Mole has finished talking | After an hour | In the morning
exercise: Why does the Mole say «(hic)»? | He has hiccups from crying | He is drunk on ale | He is laughing | He has a cold
exercise: Which way does the Rat set off? | Back the way they had come | On towards the River Bank, without a word | Into the village | Into the Wild Wood
exercise: What reasons does the Mole give against going back? | Too late, too dark, too far, snow coming | He is too tired, and the Badger would be cross with them | It's raining | His house is sold
exercise: «Hang River Bank, and supper too!» What does the Rat mean? | Never mind home and supper — this matters more | Let's hang a lantern on the river bank | Hurry up for supper | He hates the river
exercise: «If I stay out all night.» What is the Rat ready to do? | Search all night if he must | Stay out all night in the village inn | Sleep under a hedge | Give up
exercise: Why does the Rat need the Mole to be with him? | For his nose, to find the place | For his strong paws, to dig through the snow | For company | For his lantern
exercise: How does the Rat know the Mole has caught the scent? | He feels an electric thrill through his linked arm | The Mole shouts «Here it is!» at the top of his voice | The Mole points | He smells it too
exercise: What does the Rat do then? | Lets go, steps back, and waits | Runs ahead to find the house himself | Lights a lantern | Ties a rope to the Mole
exercise: «A fault—a check—a try back.» How does the Mole move? | Like a hound losing and finding a scent | In a straight line, very fast, without stopping | Backwards | Very slowly, crawling
exercise: Which of these does the Mole NOT do on the way to his home? | Swim across a stream | Cross a dry ditch | Scramble through a hedge | Cross a bare field
exercise: How does the Mole get to his front door? | He dives down a tunnel | He climbs up a big oak tree | He swims across a stream | He knocks on the side of a hill
exercise: What lights up the dark first? | A match the Mole strikes | Moonlight shining down the tunnel | A glowing firefly in the dark | The Rat's lantern, held up high
exercise: What is painted over the bell-pull? | «Mole End» | «Mr. Mole, Esquire» | «Private. No landing allowed» | «MR. BADGER»
exercise: Why does the Mole keep a roller? | He can't stand his ground kicked into little runs and heaps | To play skittles with on Sunday afternoons | To make pastry | To keep visitors out
exercise: What is in the middle of the little pond? | A cockle-shell pile with a silvered glass ball | A fountain shaped like a big fish, spouting water | A tiny boat | A statue of Queen Victoria
test: Leo: «the Rat sees Mole crying and goes 'bruh, man up'.» (bruh = brother, mate) Put it right. | He says nothing, pats him, then calls himself a pig | Right: he tells the Mole to stop crying and walk faster | He laughs at him | He goes home without him
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Rat finds Mole End himself, by looking at a map.» | False: the Mole's nose finds it | True: the Rat had a map of the fields in his pocket | True, the Rat smells it | False: the Badger shows them
test: «It's bostin' (great) — Queen Victoria is an Italian hero!» says Alfie. Is he right? | No — the writer is joking; only Garibaldi was an Italian hero | Yes, Queen Victoria was the queen of Italy | Yes, the book says so, so it's true | No, there are no statues at all
test: «It reflected everything all wrong and had a very pleasing effect.» What is funny here? | A ball that shows everything wrong is called pleasing | The ball is broken | Mole hates the ball | The ball reflects nothing
test: Why does the Rat say «carelessly» that they'd better get on, when he is really going back? | He doesn't want to make a fuss or embarrass the Mole | He has truly forgotten all about the Mole's home | He is lost | He is cross
test: Zara, a bit salty: «the Rat's mean, Mole cried and he never said sorry.» (salty = cross) Put it right. | He calls himself a pig, and then does something about it | Right: he never says or does anything kind at all | He says sorry to the Badger | He gives Mole money
test: Looking back: in chapter I, after tipping the boat over, who said sorry and how? | The Mole: «I have been a complete ass, and I know it» | The Rat: «What a pig I have been!» | The Otter, laughing | Nobody said sorry
test: So in chapter I the Mole called himself an ass; now the Rat calls himself a pig. What do both scenes show? | A good friend owns up when he is wrong | Friends should never say sorry to each other | Animals love calling names | Pigs and asses are clever
test: Looking back: when the Mole cried in chapter I, what did the Rat do? | He kindly looked in another direction | He laughed loudly and called him a baby | He told him to stop | He cried too
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what did the Rat do when the Mole went missing? | Took his pistols and cudgel and searched the Wild Wood | Waited at home by the fire until the morning came | Fetched the Badger | Went to bed
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Rat found Badger's door. Who finds the door this time? | The Mole, by his nose | The Rat again, by thinking it out step by step | The Otter, who happened by | Nobody — it was pure luck
test: Looking back: what was written on the brass plate on Badger's door in chapter III? | MR. BADGER | BADGER'S END | THE WILD WOOD | PRIVATE
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Rat called the Mole a «thick-headed beast» over the door-mat. How does the Mole look now? | Clever in his own way — his nose finds his home | Still thick-headed and slow, as before | Lazy | Rude
test: What would have happened if the Rat had kept going home? | Mole End would stay lost, and the Mole would stay sad | The Mole would have run back home on his own that night | The Badger would have found it | Nothing
test: ★★★ «Too late, too dark, too far off, and the snow's coming!» How many «too»s does the Mole use in this cry? | 3 | 4 | 2 | 1
test: ★★★ «Garibaldi, and the infant Samuel, and Queen Victoria, and other heroes of modern Italy.» Which word shows the writer's trick? | «other» — it pretends they are all Italian heroes | «infant» — Samuel was a baby | «plaster» — they are fake | «modern» — they are all new
test: ★★★ «He had lost what he could hardly be said to have found», last lesson. What does the Rat's turning back now mean for that? | The Mole will find it properly after all | The Mole has lost it for ever and ever | The Rat has lost his supper and his house | Nothing changes
exam: Identify the occasion upon which the Mole's grief became manifest to the Rat. | A rest upon some tree-stumps beside a copse | The arrival of the two animals at the River Bank, late at night | Their passage through the village | Their encounter with the Otter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Rat's response upon learning the cause of the Mole's distress? | Self-reproach, followed by a resolute return along the road | Indifference, and a continuation of the journey towards the River Bank without delay | Anger towards the Mole | Laughter
exam: It is given that the Mole's dwelling was located by scent. Ascertain the faculty employed. | The Mole's nose | The Rat's knowledge of the district, gained over many seasons | A map of the fields | The Otter's directions
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the inscription above the bell-pull. | «Mole End», in Gothic lettering | «Mr. Mole», engraved upon a brass plate in square capital letters | «Private» | No inscription
